			"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@jamesarosen/110431722735606744",
			"author": {
				"name": "James A Rosen",
				"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@jamesarosen",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/110/186/788/652/673/617/original/3dbebb11d8e01b7d.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2023-05-25T22:39:23.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I found Preact’s signals to be *very* difficult to use for two reasons:</p><p>1. It’s hard to mix with reactive code, so it’s hard to gradually migrate an existing code base</p><p>2. There’s no destructor / unwatch hook. That means I can’t build complex classes out of signals and treat them like signals. That, in turn, makes it hard to mix signals with imperative APIs like graphs.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
